         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Images from Caral\n\nThese images are copyright for Caral and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"ChronologyCaralPyramid\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('ChronologyCaralPyramid', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Pyramid H1 at the archaeological site of Caral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Culture and Civilization - Is there a hierarchy between them? (<htcite2>)</li><li>Jōmon Period (<htdate1>/<htdate2> - <htdate3>) - initial historical period characterized by vessels with cord markings (jōmon). (<htcite3>)</li><li>Higher orders with Soroban and Suanpan (算盤) - In this text, we learn how to represent orders higher than the units in the different versions of the abacus. (<htcite4>)</li><li>Bigger or Smaller - Concluding the comparisons, we analyze the relationship between objects by classifying them as bigger or smaller. (<htcite5>)</li><li>Learning from nature - This text explores how knowledge of multiplication could have helped ancient civilizations. (<htcite6>)</li><li>Texts with Sciences (Part III) - With everything we have learned, what conclusions can we draw from ancient texts that discuss deluges? (<htcite7>)</li><li>Returning - In this text, we present the subtraction operation as a form of return. (<htcite8>)</li><li>Chronology - Is it possible to create a universal timeline applicable to all of humanity? (<htcite9>)</li><li>Cradles of civilization (Part I) - While humanity originated in Africa, the first civilizations emerged in various regions around the world, such as Mesopotamia, the Andes, and Mesoamerica. (<htcite10>)</li><li>Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1) - Many times, the multiplication table is presented as something to be memorized — but, why should we ignore the knowledge that comes with it? (<htcite23>)</li><li>Simplicity vs. Complexity - Despite more than 13.8 billion years of evolution of the universe, we often still wish to be like it was in its beginnings. Why? (<htcite24>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"img0\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img0', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Section of panel found in archaeological site of Caral, courtesy of © ZonaArqueologicaCaral (<htcite11> ; <htcite12>). Photo taken on <htdate4>, during a visit to the site.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Beyond fingers - Exploring the limitations and the necessity of new tools to count. (<htcite13>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">3 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgQuipuPanel\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgQuipuPanel', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Panel at the archaeological site of Caral, ©ZonaArqueologicaCaral, (<htcite14> ; <htcite15>). Photo taken on <htdate5>, during a visit to the site.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>The Writing System - More than just a tool for daily use, the writing system is a milestone that allows us to divide human history into prehistory and recorded events. (<htcite16>)</li><li>Mesoamerican sums - We studied how the Mesoamericans might have used addition in their numerical system. (<htcite17>)</li><li>Learning from nature - This text explores how knowledge of multiplication could have helped ancient civilizations. (<htcite18>)</li><li>What do I need to build? - The question may seem complex, but the answer might be right around you. (<htcite19>)</li><li>The Quipu: numbers in order - Learn how the Quipu represents numbers with knots and why the order of each knot gives a number its precise value. (<htcite20>)</li><li>Cradles of civilization (Part I) - While humanity originated in Africa, the first civilizations emerged in various regions around the world, such as Mesopotamia, the Andes, and Mesoamerica. (<htcite21>)</li><li>Change of direction (Part I) - We study the logic behind the different approach used by indigenous communities as their numbers grow.. (<htcite22>)</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
